index 0000000..482c8d2
--- /dev/null
+++ b/HEGEL_REPORT.md
@@ -0,0 +1,77 @@
+# Hegel Property-Based Testing Report: `ron` 0.12.2
+
+Tests were added with `hegeltest` 0.28.2 (added with `cargo add --dev hegeltest --ignore-rust-version`; ron pins `rust-version = 1.64.0`, hegeltest needs 1.86 — a dev-dependency MSRV concern for maintainers to weigh).
+
+Per the skill, all properties were added to *existing* test files (`tests/value.rs`, `tests/escape.rs`, `tests/307_stack_overflow.rs`, `tests/min_max.rs`, `tests/extensions.rs`), each inside a `mod hegel_props`.
+
+## Properties tested
+
+### `tests/value.rs` — untyped `Value` roundtrips (the crown)
+
+A recursive generator `arbitrary_value(depth)` (via `compose!` + `.boxed()`, depth-bounded to 4, well below the recursion limit of 128) covers bool/char/number (all integer widths + f32/f64)/string/bytes/option (incl. nested `Some(None)`)/seq/map/unit, with map keys deduplicated *by parse-normalized form* (keys `U8(1)` and `I64(1)` both serialize as `1` and would collapse when re-parsed). String/char leaves are drawn from an escape-boundary-boosted char generator.
+
+1. **`prop_value_roundtrip_compact`** — `from_str::<Value>(&to_string(&v))` preserves the value, compared through a `Canon` form (numbers compared by numeric value + int-vs-float distinction, NaN by sign only), and the roundtrip reaches a fixpoint after one parse (`v2 == v3` exactly). Evidence: the crate's own fuzz target (`fuzz/fuzz_targets/from_str.rs`) does `from_str::<Value>` → `to_string`; `tests/337_value_float_roundtrip.rs` and `map_roundtrip_338` assert instances of this property. Canonical comparison is required because the untyped parser deliberately normalizes number types (`"42"` → `U8`, exactly-f32-representable floats → `F32`; `src/parse.rs` `ParsedFloat` selection). **Found 2 real bugs (below).**
+2. **`prop_pretty_and_compact_parse_to_same_value`** — `to_string_pretty` under an arbitrary generated `PrettyConfig` (depth_limit, new_line `\n`/`\r\n`/`\r`, exotic-whitespace indentors/separators, separate_tuple_members, enumerate_arrays, compact_arrays/structs/maps, escape_strings, number_suffixes, compact_ranges, struct_names) parses back to the same value as compact serialization. Configuration is generated, not canned, per the skill's "generate configurations, not just payloads". Extensions are deliberately excluded here (`IMPLICIT_SOME` legitimately changes what an untyped re-parse yields).
+3. **`prop_whitespace_and_comments_do_not_change_parse`** — a custom emitter re-serializes the `Value` with random whitespace (all `ws_single` chars from `docs/grammar.md`, incl. `\u{200e}`, `\u{2028}`…), line comments, nested block comments, and random trailing commas between all tokens; the parse must equal the compact parse. Evidence: `docs/grammar.md` `ws`/comment grammar, `tests/comments.rs`, `tests/410_trailing_comma.rs`.
+
+Plus the two deterministic known-bug pins (below).
+
+### `tests/escape.rs` — escape-set-boundary roundtrips
+
+4. **`prop_string_escape_roundtrip`** — strings built from characters at the boundary of `serialize_escaped_str`'s escape set (`char::escape_debug`: quotes, backslash, controls, `\u{7f}`, `\u{85}`, `\u{2028}/\u{2029}`, combining `\u{301}`, BOM, `\u{10ffff}`, plus uniform codepoints) roundtrip through `to_string`/`from_str`. Evidence: `test_ascii_chars`, `test_nul_in_string`, `tests/425_escape_strings.rs`.
+5. **`prop_char_escape_roundtrip`** — same for `char` literals (`serialize_char` escapes only `'` and `\`, so raw controls/newlines inside `'…'` are exercised).
+6. **`prop_unescaped_raw_string_roundtrip`** — with `PrettyConfig::escape_strings(false)`, strings biased toward `"`/`#`/`\`/line breaks roundtrip via the raw-string fallback, targeting `serialize_unescaped_or_raw_str`'s hash-count selection (longest `#` run + 1).
+
+### `tests/307_stack_overflow.rs` — parser robustness
+
+7. **`prop_from_str_never_panics_on_arbitrary_text`** — `from_str::<Value>` on arbitrary unicode text returns without panicking. Evidence: the fuzz target's contract.
+8. **`prop_from_str_never_panics_on_ron_token_soup`** — same for concatenations of RON-suggestive token fragments (`r#"`, `br#"`, `0x`, `\u{`, `/*`, suffixes, …), which reach much deeper into the parser than uniform text.
+9. **`prop_deeply_nested_input_errors_gracefully`** — nesting (`[`, `(…,)`, `Some(…)`, `{0:…}`) swept explicitly to depth 5000 (per the skill: draw depth directly, don't hope a generator wanders deep). Depth ≤ 30 must parse; depth > 128 must fail with exactly `Error::ExceededRecursionLimit` (not a stack overflow); in between, the recursion limit is the only permitted error. Evidence: `Options::recursion_limit` docs (default 128), existing `test_deeply_nested_map` and fuzz regression tests in this file.
+10. **`prop_deeply_nested_value_serialization_errors_gracefully`** — the serializer-side mirror: a `Value` nested past the limit (built iteratively) must return `Err(ExceededRecursionLimit)`, one within it must serialize; the exact 127/128/129 boundary is boosted via `one_of!`. Evidence: `test_deeply_nested_struct`, `guard_recursion!` in `src/ser/mod.rs`.
+
+### `tests/min_max.rs` — number boundaries
+
+11. **`prop_integer_roundtrip`** — typed roundtrip for all eight integer widths with MIN/MAX/0/1 boosted alongside the full range. Evidence: the existing `test_*_min/max` unit tests generalized.
+12. **`prop_f64_roundtrip`** — bit-exact typed f64 roundtrip incl. MIN/MAX, subnormals (5e-324), ±inf, ±0.0, 2^53+1; NaN checked for NaN-ness + sign (the text form `NaN`/`-NaN` cannot carry payloads; `serialize_f64` explicitly writes the NaN sign). Evidence: `src/ser/mod.rs` float serialization, `tests/floats.rs`.
+13. **`prop_f32_roundtrip`** — same for f32.
+14. **`prop_untyped_integer_value_preserved`** — untyped parse of any i64/u64 decimal text yields an *integer* `Number` variant with the same `i128` value (int-vs-float distinction and value preservation, independent of which width the parser picks).
+
+### `tests/extensions.rs` — extension flags
+
+15. **`prop_roundtrip_under_all_extension_combinations`** — generated `Struct` data (the file's existing struct with unit/newtype/tuple structs, `Vec<Option<…>>`, enum map values) roundtrips under *every* subset of `Extensions::all()` (`unwrap_newtypes` × `implicit_some` × `unwrap_variant_newtypes` × `explicit_struct_names`), drawn as raw bits. Evidence: the powerset loop in `src/extensions.rs`'s own `test_extension_serde` (which only roundtrips the flags value, not data under the flags), `tests/roundtrip.rs`.
+
+## Test results
+
+`cargo test --no-fail-fast`: **471 passed, 2 failed** — the two failures are the deterministic known-bug pins, kept failing on purpose:
+
+- `hegel_props::known_bug_untyped_f32_value_roundtrip_changes_value` (tests/value.rs) — FAILS (real bug #1)
+- `hegel_props::known_bug_f32_exact_f64_roundtrip_not_stable` (tests/value.rs) — FAILS (real bug #2, same root cause)
+
+All 15 properties pass at the default 100 cases and at a 10× exploratory run (`test_cases = 1000`, temporarily applied and reverted; case count verified with an atomic counter reaching 1000, not wall-clock). Liveness was verified by mutation: every property's assertion was inverted (all 15 reported shrunk counterexamples), and each guarded branch was mutated separately to confirm it fires — the shallow (`depth = 1`), middle (`depth = 65`), and deep parse branches, both serializer branches, and both float NaN branches.
+
+## Suspected bugs
+
+Both pins are one root cause with two reachability levels: **serializing an `F32` inside a `Value` uses `f32`'s shortest-roundtrip `Display`, but the untyped parser re-reads the text as `f64`** (`src/ser/mod.rs::serialize_f32` vs `src/parse.rs` float parsing / `ParsedFloat` selection, which only downgrades to `F32` when the parsed `f64` is exactly `f32`-representable).
+
+1. **User-constructed `F32` values change numeric value across a roundtrip** (`known_bug_untyped_f32_value_roundtrip_changes_value`). Minimal counterexample: `Value::Number(Number::F32(0.1))` → `"0.1"` → `Value::Number(F64(0.1_f64))`. `0.1_f64` (0.1000000000000000055…) ≠ `f64::from(0.1_f32)` (0.1000000014901161…) — the *numeric value* drifts, not just the variant tag. Hegel's original shrunk find was `F32(33644950.0)`. Oracle-independent (a roundtrip failing against the input still in hand), so almost certainly real. This affects most `f32` values with fractional parts.
+
+2. **The bug is reachable purely through ron's own parser — parse→serialize→parse is not a fixpoint** (`known_bug_f32_exact_f64_roundtrip_not_stable`): `"33644952.0"` parses to `F32(33644952.0)` (exactly f32-representable, so the parser downgrades); `f32::Display` prints the shortest decimal rounding to that f32, `"33644950"`; re-parsing gives `F64(33644950.0)` — the value drifted by 2.0 with no user-constructed `F32` anywhere. Found by the fixpoint half of `prop_value_roundtrip_compact` after the first bug was excluded.
+
+Suggested fix (not applied, per instructions): serialize `F32` using the shortest decimal that roundtrips *as `f64`* of `f64::from(x)` — the parser's exact-representability downgrade would then restore `F32(x)` losslessly. The crate's own fuzz target (`from_str` → `to_string` without comparison) cannot see either bug.
+
+The generator for the sibling properties excludes the drifting region via `f32_survives_untyped_roundtrip` / `f64_untyped_roundtrip_is_stable`, with comments referencing the pinned tests.
+
+## Unsound properties abandoned
+
+- **Exact `Value` equality after the *first* roundtrip** — unsound: the untyped parser normalizes number *types* by design (`"42"` → `U8` regardless of the serialized width; test `337_value_float_roundtrip.rs` documents `"1.0"` → `F32`). Replaced with canonical (value-semantics) comparison plus exact equality only from the second roundtrip on.
+- **"Depth ≤ 128 parses / depth > 128 errors" with an exact cutoff** — first formulation failed at depth 65: the deserializer's `guard_recursion!` consumes more than one frame per syntactic nesting level, so the exact trip point is an implementation detail. Weakened to safe distances (≤ 30 parses, > 128 errors, in between only `ExceededRecursionLimit` permitted). Interestingly the *serializer's* guard does count exactly one frame per level (the 127/128/129 boundary assertion holds).
+- **Map key sets compared positionally** — `Value`'s `Ord` orders by `Number` variant before value, so numerically-equal-but-differently-typed keys reorder across a roundtrip; `Canon::Map` sorts entries canonically instead. Duplicate-after-normalization keys (e.g. `U8(1)` and `I64(1)`) are excluded in the generator since RON text cannot represent them as distinct keys.
+
+## Skill feedback
+
+- **The porting/reference docs were accurate; near-zero API friction.** The `compose!`-plus-`BoxedGenerator<'static, _>` recipe for recursive generators worked first try, as did `tuples!`, `one_of!`, `sampled_from` of tuples, and `.map` on integer generators for chars. The documented gotchas (no bare `generators::integers()`, `Generator` import only when using combinators) matched reality.
+- **`cargo add --dev hegeltest` MSRV failure and the `--ignore-rust-version` retry** happened exactly as the Setup section predicts (ron pins 1.64.0). Having that in the reference saved a debugging cycle.
+- **`generators::just(T)` requires `Clone + Send + Sync`** — hit E0599 with a non-`Clone` unit struct (`generators::optional(generators::just(UnitStruct))`); the reference documents the bounds but an explicit "for field-less structs just draw `booleans()` and map" hint would have been faster than the compiler error, which is verbose and points at `OptionalGenerator`/`JustGenerator` internals rather than the missing `Clone`.
+- **`cargo test` fail-fast interacts badly with the mutation liveness check**: with mutations across five test binaries, plain `cargo test` stops after the first failing binary, which initially looked like only 4 of 15 mutations had taken. The skill's Run-and-Reflect section could mention `--no-fail-fast` for the "break all properties" verification pass.
+- **The "guarded branches must fire" advice was load-bearing**: mutating both the shallow and deep branches of the nesting property in one run only proved the *shallow* branch fires (shrinking reports the minimal failure, masking the other branch); each guard needed its own mutation run. A sentence noting "one mutation run per guarded branch — shrinking will hide all but the minimal one" would sharpen the existing advice.
+- **The "generate at the encode-set boundary" and fixpoint guidance directly produced both bug finds**: the first failure came from broad float generation, but recognizing the *second* manifestation required the "second roundtrip is the identity" clause, which is not spelled out in the roundtrip pattern — consider adding "for normalizing parsers, also assert the roundtrip stabilizes after one pass" to the Roundtrip catalogue entry, since that is what proved the bug reachable without user-constructed values.
